{% from "partials/nav-macros.njk" import sectionList %}
{#
  The three overlays, plus the section jump list. All are <dialog> elements, so
  focus trapping, inertness and Escape come from the browser rather than from
  hand-written key handling. overlay.js wires them.

  Every one closes three ways: the close button, a tap on the dimmed area
  around it, and Escape. Keyboard users get all three; a phone user tapping
  away from a sheet gets the one they expect.
#}

{% macro closeButton(label) %}
  <button type="button" class="overlay-close" data-overlay-close aria-label="Close {{ label }}">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path d="M4 4l8 8M12 4l-8 8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    </svg>
  </button>
{% endmacro %}

{# ------------------------------------------------ command palette / search #}
<dialog class="palette" data-palette aria-label="Search and jump to">
  <div class="palette__inner">
    <div class="palette__field">
      <svg class="palette__icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <circle cx="9" cy="9" r="5.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>
        <path d="M13.2 13.2 17 17" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
      </svg>
      <label class="visually-hidden" for="palette-input">Search and jump to</label>
      <input
        class="palette__input"
        id="palette-input"
        type="text"
        autocomplete="off"
        spellcheck="false"
        placeholder="Search"
        role="combobox"
        aria-expanded="true"
        aria-controls="palette-results"
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        data-palette-input>
      {{ closeButton("search") }}
    </div>

    {#
      Where the search is looking, and the way out of it.

      The scope is not something the reader chose — it is a consequence of the
      page they opened the search from — so it is stated plainly rather than
      left to be inferred from the results. It sits under the field and not at
      the foot of the panel because "where am I searching" is a question asked
      on opening, not after scrolling past forty results, and the way to widen
      it is a button beside the statement rather than a mode spelled into the
      query, so the field only ever holds what the reader typed.

      Hidden off a game, where there is nothing to widen and the placeholder
      has already said so.
    #}
    <div class="palette__scope" data-palette-scope hidden>
      <p class="palette__scope-note" data-palette-scope-note aria-live="polite"></p>
      <button type="button" class="palette__scope-toggle" data-palette-scope-toggle
              data-palette-scope-action></button>
    </div>

    {#
      Only the results scroll. The field and the scope bar stay put, because
      what you are searching and where you are searching it are the two things
      you need while reading a long list — and because a scrollbar running the
      full height of the panel cuts across its rounded corners.
    #}
    <div class="palette__scroll">
      <p class="palette__empty" data-palette-empty></p>

      <ul class="palette__results" id="palette-results" role="listbox"
          aria-label="Results" data-palette-results></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</dialog>

{# ------------------------------------------------------- bookmarks drawer #}
<dialog class="drawer" data-bookmarks-drawer aria-labelledby="drawer-title">
  <div class="drawer__inner">
    <header class="drawer__header">
      <h2 class="drawer__title" id="drawer-title">Bookmarks</h2>
      <div class="drawer__actions">
        <button type="button" class="drawer__scope" data-drawer-scope aria-pressed="false">
          Show All Games
        </button>
        {{ closeButton("bookmarks") }}
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="drawer__empty" data-drawer-empty hidden></p>
    <div class="drawer__body" data-drawer-body></div>
  </div>
</dialog>

{# --------------------------------------------------- reading preferences #}
<dialog class="prefs" data-preferences-panel aria-labelledby="prefs-title">
  <div class="prefs__inner">
    <header class="drawer__header">
      <h2 class="drawer__title" id="prefs-title">Reading</h2>
      <div class="drawer__actions">
        <button type="button" class="drawer__scope" data-preferences-reset>Reset</button>
        {{ closeButton("reading preferences") }}
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prefs__body" data-preferences-body></div>

    {#
      Everything the site has stored, under one label like the settings above
      it — but set apart by a rule, because these are not settings. Every
      control above changes how a page looks; every control here moves or
      destroys what the reader has made.
    #}
    <section class="prefs__section" aria-labelledby="prefs-data-label">
      <p class="pref-group__label" id="prefs-data-label">Your data</p>

      {#
        The note has to name the cost, not just the privacy. "Saved in this
        browser only" reads as reassurance — nothing is uploaded — and buries
        the part a reader would want to act on, which is that a browser is a
        place things get deleted from.
      #}
      <p class="prefs__note">
        Saved in this browser only: nothing is uploaded and nothing is shared
        with your other devices. Clearing your browsing data removes it, and
        Safari deletes it after about a week without a visit.
      </p>

      {#
        Taking it with you, above the clear buttons — "keep a copy" should be
        the easier of the two things to find in a panel that also destroys
        data. And the history is why the clearing is here at all: bookmarks are
        things the reader chose to keep, but the history is a record the site
        wrote down on their behalf, and that should never be something only the
        developer can erase.
      #}
      <div class="prefs__data">
        <button type="button" class="prefs__transfer" data-export>Download a copy</button>
        <button type="button" class="prefs__transfer" data-import-open>Restore from a file</button>
        <input class="visually-hidden" type="file" accept="application/json,.json"
               data-import-file tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
      </div>

      <p class="prefs__result" data-transfer-result role="status" hidden></p>

      <div class="prefs__data">
        <button type="button" class="prefs__clear" data-clear-favorites>Clear favorites</button>
        <button type="button" class="prefs__clear" data-clear-history>Clear history</button>
        <button type="button" class="prefs__clear" data-clear-bookmarks>Clear bookmarks</button>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</dialog>

{# ------------------------------- section jump list, opened by the sticky bar #}
{% if contentType and contentType.sections.length %}
  <dialog class="jump-sheet" data-section-jump aria-label="Jump to a section in {{ contentType.title }}">
    <div class="jump-sheet__inner">
      <header class="drawer__header">
        <h2 class="drawer__title">{{ contentType.title }}</h2>
        <div class="drawer__actions">
          {{ closeButton("section list") }}
        </div>
      </header>
      <div class="jump-sheet__body">
        {{ sectionList(contentType, "jump-list jump-list--sheet") }}
      </div>
    </div>
  </dialog>
{% endif %}
